---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import CaseStudyProse from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudyProse.astro";
import CaseStudySection from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudySection.astro";
import CaseStudyCards from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudyCards.astro";
import CaseStudyCard from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudyCard.astro";

import heroImage from "../../assets/images/the-gallimaufry/home.png";
import episodeImage from "../../assets/images/the-gallimaufry/episode.png";
import contactImage from "../../assets/images/the-gallimaufry/contact.png";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
	title="Building The Gallimaufry website"
	pageTitle="The Gallimaufry website case study - Will Jackson"
	description="How I built a fast, self-updating website for The Gallimaufry history podcast in Astro and TypeScript, with episode pages generated from the Buzzsprout API."
	path="/work/the-gallimaufry/"
	lede="A fast, low-maintenance website for the history podcast I co-host. It builds itself from the show's Buzzsprout feed, so publishing an episode never means updating the website by hand."
	facts={[
		{ label: "Client", value: "The Gallimaufry, a history podcast I co-host" },
		{ label: "Role", value: "Design, development and hosting" },
		{ label: "Stack", value: "Astro, TypeScript, Buzzsprout API, Lettermint, Dokploy" },
		{ label: "Year", value: "2020 to present" },
	]}
	heroImage={heroImage}
	heroImageAlt="The Gallimaufry homepage, with the show's hourglass logo and the latest episodes"
	link="https://thegallimaufry.show"
>
	<CaseStudyProse heading="The brief">
		<p>
			Podcast hosts give every show a basic web page, but it doesn't look
			like the show, it isn't yours to shape, and it does little for
			search. We wanted a proper home for The Gallimaufry, with a page for
			every episode that people could find and share.
		</p>
		<p>
			It also had to look after itself. Neither of us wanted another job
			on release day, so a new episode had to appear on the site without
			anyone touching it, and the site had to be cheap and simple to host.
		</p>
	</CaseStudyProse>

	<CaseStudySection
		heading="Episodes straight from Buzzsprout"
		image={episodeImage}
		imageAlt="An episode page with an audio player, episode notes, artwork and links to listening apps"
	>
		<p>
			When the site builds, it fetches every episode from the Buzzsprout
			API and turns each one into its own page, with a player, show notes,
			artwork, a readable date and length, and a clean address made from
			the title. The build stops with a clear error if two episodes would
			ever share an address, so a page can never go missing quietly.
		</p>
		<p>
			Each page carries PodcastEpisode structured data and links to Apple
			Podcasts, Spotify, Amazon Music and RSS. Navigation uses Astro's
			view transitions, and I fixed a browser quirk that left audio
			players silent until a full reload after moving between pages.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection
		heading="A contact form that stops spam, not listeners"
		image={contactImage}
		imageAlt="The contact form, with name, email and message fields and a Cloudflare Turnstile check"
		reverse
	>
		<p>
			Every message goes through a short screening before it's sent: a
			hidden honeypot field, a minimum time to fill in the form, a check
			for random-string names and messages, and finally Cloudflare
			Turnstile. The cheap checks run first, so Turnstile is only called
			when they pass.
		</p>
		<p>
			Spam is dropped silently, so bots learn nothing. But if the check
			itself can't run, the sender is told and given the show's email
			address instead, because losing a real listener's message is worse
			than letting some spam through. Genuine messages are delivered by
			Lettermint.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudyCards>
		<CaseStudyCard heading="Privacy by default">
			<p>
				Google Analytics doesn't load until a visitor accepts it in the
				cookie banner, and consent is denied by default, so nothing
				reaches Google from someone who hasn't said yes.
			</p>
		</CaseStudyCard>

		<CaseStudyCard heading="Tested and self-hosted">
			<p>
				Vitest covers the episode catalogue, spam screening and the
				form's browser code, and GitHub Actions runs the tests and a type
				check on every pull request. The site ships as a Docker image to
				my own server through Dokploy, with secrets passed in at build
				time rather than baked into the image.
			</p>
		</CaseStudyCard>
	</CaseStudyCards>
</CaseStudyLayout>
